    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-25866",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178864/assets/10cd6e85765f6b79ab4bdab95f9ba9c34b012529b9bcd5d3cbdb93765cd99d6a.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is an octagon (it has eight sides). So, the polygons $R,$ $T,$ and $Q$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $8$ sides.\n\n- The polygon $S$ is *not* congruent to $\\,P$ (even though they both have $8$ sides) because $S$ and $P$ don't have the same shape. In particular, notice that $P$ has a right angle, while $S$ doesn't have a right angle.\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $U$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅U.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
